# Listening Notes — environment variables.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values. Nothing here is secret;
# it is the list of what a copy can be told, so it lives in the repo. .env.local
# is gitignored and never leaves your machine.
#
# On Vercel these go in Project → Settings → Environment Variables instead.
#
# Editing .env.local: use a terminal, not VS Code. VS Code silently fails to
# save dotfiles in this project. Check your work with `cat .env.local`.
#
# ── Only one of these is required ─────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE_URL. Everything else is either set by the deploy, generated by the
# copy on its first start, or asked for on the site — during setup, or later
# in Settings. The variables below still work for a copy that already set
# them, and a value typed into Settings wins over the variable, because it is
# the later decision.

# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your Neon Postgres connection string.
# Neon dashboard → your project → Connection Details → "Connection string".
# Include ?sslmode=require. The deploy button's Neon integration sets this for
# you; it is only typed by hand for a copy run some other way.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=require

# ── Set by the deploy, never by hand ──────────────────────────────────────

# The same database at its direct address, without Neon's connection pooler.
# Vercel's Neon integration sets it beside DATABASE_URL. The migrator prefers
# it, because the lock it takes lives on one database session and the pooler
# does not promise one; where it is missing, the `-pooler` marker is stripped
# off DATABASE_URL instead, so a hand-run copy can leave this blank.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=

# Who gave you this journal, when it arrived as a gift. /get's button fills it
# in on the new project when its gift card is showing; setup reads it once,
# after the claim, to offer adding the giver to your address book and waving.
# Nothing else reads it, and there is no reason to set it yourself.
GIFT_FROM=

# ── Optional: things the site normally handles itself ─────────────────────

# Signs the login cookie. Generated on first start and kept in the copy's own
# database if this is blank, which is the normal case — it is a random key,
# and no person should have to invent one.
#
# Set it only if you want to choose it. Changing it retires every session
# cookie issued under the old one, so you are signed out on every device and
# log in again with the same password. That is the whole cost — disruptive,
# not dangerous. Don't rotate it casually; do rotate it if you think it leaked.
SESSION_SECRET=

# The password you type to reach the writing side of your own journal.
# Chosen on the site during setup, and changed in Settings; leave this blank.
# A copy that was locked this way before setup asked keeps working, and can
# move the password onto the site from Settings at any time.
SESSION_PASSWORD=

# Retired. This was the Claude API key behind album research and the question
# mark on the cover; both came out of the software on 2026-09-18
# (docs/RETIRED-PROMPTS.md), and nothing reads the key for any feature now.
# The variable and the secrets column stay, because the schema is additive-only
# and a copy that set one should know what it was. Leave it blank.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── Optional: this copy's own business ────────────────────────────────────

# Where `npm run backup` writes its snapshots.
# Defaults to ~/listening-notes-backups if unset.
#
# Point it at a synced folder and your backups stop living on the same machine
# as the thing they protect — a laptop that dies otherwise takes its own
# backups with it. On macOS, iCloud Drive is:
#
#   BACKUP_DIR=~/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/listening-notes-backups
#
# A sync that is signed out or out of space fails silently: the local write
# still succeeds and the log still says everything worked.
#
# Every snapshot holds the secrets table too — the password hash and the key
# that signs you in — so keep the folder as private as your .env.local.
BACKUP_DIR=

# Where this copy's source code lives.
#
# Only set this if you have MODIFIED the code. The AGPL asks a network-run copy
# to offer the source of the program actually running, which for a fork is your
# repository rather than the original — so point this at yours.
#
# Leave it blank on an unmodified copy. The default is upstream, which is the
# honest answer, and nobody should have to think about licence compliance to
# keep a listening journal.
#
# It is read in the browser, which is why it carries the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix:
# the link is shown to visitors, who are the people the offer is owed to.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOURCE_URL=

# Where "Report a problem" sends what a keeper writes on their desk.
#
# The default is the copy the software comes from, the same on every copy, and
# it is right for an unmodified one. A fork that would rather receive its own
# copies' reports points this at its own /api/reports. Read in the browser,
# hence the prefix.
NEXT_PUBLIC_REPORTS_URL=

# ── Read, but not yours to set ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Vercel provides VERCEL_URL, VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL and the VERCEL_GIT_*
# variables to every build; the claim link in the build log and the desk's
# "is this copy updating itself" line read them, and nothing needs doing. The
# update workflow's script (scripts/update_copy.mjs) reads UPSTREAM_URL inside
# GitHub Actions, not here.
